 "what_happened": "Mr Treharne, then 74, has held an account with an online lottery website, LuckyLine, since 2019. On 3 February 2022 he received a call from a man who introduced himself as 'Graham Fairbanks, prize reconciliation officer'. Mr Fairbanks said LuckyLine's records showed a deceased player with the same name and date of birth as Mr Treharne whose jackpot of £940,000 had never been claimed, and that under 'namesake rules' the prize could be released to him once a series of fees had been paid. He was told not to log in to his LuckyLine account while the claim was 'in escrow'. Over the following eight months Mr Treharne paid what he was told were release fees, an anti-money-laundering certificate, 'international transfer insurance' and a 'court stamp' - 18 payments in total - to an account held by Kestrel Claims Bureau Ltd. When he first set up the payee on 9 February 2022, Thamesgate's Confirmation of Payee check returned 'no match' against the name he typed ('LuckyLine Prize Release'); he went ahead after Mr Fairbanks told him the release desk used a contractor. He received two credits from Kestrel, which he understood to be 'partial releases'. Contact became sporadic after October 2022, but Mr Treharne carried on e-mailing Mr Fairbanks until December 2023. He says he realised it was a scam in January 2024, when a friend showed him a newspaper article about 'namesake' lottery frauds, and he then instructed Brightwater Redress.",
