 "account_history_note": "Statements for the twelve months before February 2022 show day-to-day spending of between £20 and £600, a standing order of £300 a month to Mr Treharne's daughter, quarterly payments of about £180 to a water company, and one larger payment of £4,950 to a car dealership in August 2021. The scam payments were funded mainly by transfers from his Thamesgate cash ISA.",
 "bank_final_response_extract": "We are sorry to hear what happened to your client. We have reviewed the claim under the CRM Code. We consider that your client made these payments without a reasonable basis for believing he was dealing with a legitimate business: he was contacted out of the blue, the story he was told was implausible, he did not check his LuckyLine account, and our Confirmation of Payee check told him that the account name did not match. We also do not consider that any of the payments was unusual enough for us to show an Effective Warning: they were spread over eight months and the largest was not significantly out of line with your client's previous account use. We contacted the receiving bank but no funds remained. We are therefore unable to reimburse your client.",
 "representative_submissions": "Dear Sir or Madam. We act for Mr Alwyn Treharne in respect of losses of £22,463.50 to an authorised push payment scam. Our client is a vulnerable consumer. At the time of the payments he was in his seventies, he has never owned a smartphone and he has very limited knowledge of technology or of online fraud. Tragically, our client also suffered the loss of his sister during the course of the scam, which left him emotionally exposed to the scammer's continued pressure. Under R2(3) of the CRM Code he should be reimbursed in full, regardless of any exception the bank seeks to rely on. In the alternative, Thamesgate should have intervened from payment 9 onwards, when the frequency and value of the payments increased, and should refund at least 50% of every payment from that point, with 8% simple interest. We note that Thamesgate has not provided any record of having asked our client about his personal circumstances when he reported the scam. Brightwater Redress Ltd is authorised and regulated by the Financial Conduct Authority. This letter has been generated from our case management system; please quote our reference in all correspondence.",
 "customer_emails_to_scammer": [
  {
   "date": "2022-03-15",
   "text": "Mr Fairbanks, the £650 arrived today, thank you. So it is real after all. What is the next fee?"
  },
  {
   "date": "2022-05-10",
   "text": "I have moved money over from my ISA for the certificate. I will send the £6,450 tomorrow morning. Please make sure the full amount is with me by the end of May as you promised."
  },
  {
   "date": "2022-10-03",
   "text": "That was the last £500 I can manage. Please do not ask me for any more."
  },
  {
   "date": "2023-06-19",
   "text": "Still nothing. You told me the court stamp was the final step."
  },
  {
   "date": "2023-12-04",
   "text": "Mr Fairbanks, I have heard nothing from you since the summer. We buried my sister Gwenllian last month and I cannot take any more of this. Please just send what is owed."
  }
 ],
 "investigator_view_extract": "I'm satisfied Thamesgate can fairly rely on the reasonable basis exception. The contact came out of the blue, the namesake story wasn't plausible, Mr Treharne was told not to check his own LuckyLine account, Confirmation of Payee returned 'no match', and it's unclear why so many separate fees were needed. Most of the payments were modest and spread over eight months, so I don't think Thamesgate needed to warn Mr Treharne about them. But payment 11 - £6,450 on 11 May 2022 - was the largest payment by some margin, came two and five days after payments of £1,850 and £2,300 to the same recently added payee, and was higher than anything Mr Treharne had paid in the previous year. I think Thamesgate should have identified a scam risk and given an Effective Warning at that point, and it gave no warning at all. Where an exception applies but the firm has also failed to meet its standards, the CRM Code shares the loss, so Thamesgate should refund 50% of payment 11. As Mr Treharne received money back from Kestrel, I think it is fair to deduct the total of those credits from payment 11 first and then apply the 50%. I don't think the later payments needed further warnings: they were smaller and the payee was by then established. I've also considered R2(3). Mr Treharne's age alone doesn't make him vulnerable to this scam, and the scam was carried out by phone calls and e-mails, so I don't think his limited confidence with technology stopped him protecting himself. I'm sorry to learn of the death of Mr Treharne's sister; I asked Brightwater for more detail about it but have not received any. Thamesgate should add interest at 8% simple a year from the date it declined the claim to the date of settlement.",
 "code_extracts": {
  "R2(1)(c)": "In all the circumstances at the time of the payment, in particular the characteristics of the Customer and the complexity and sophistication of the APP scam, the Customer made the payment without a reasonable basis for believing that: i. the payee was the person the Customer was expecting to pay; ii. the payment was for genuine goods or services; and/or iii. the person or business with whom they transacted was legitimate.",
  "R2(3)": "A Customer is vulnerable to APP scams if it would not be reasonable to expect that Customer to have protected themselves, at the time of becoming victim of an APP scam, against that particular APP scam, to the extent of the impact they suffered. This should be assessed on a case-by-case basis. In these circumstances, the Customer should be reimbursed notwithstanding the provisions in R2(1), and whether or not the Firm had previously identified the Customer as vulnerable."
 },
